  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$f(x)=\\frac{|x-1|}{x^{2}}=\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}\\frac{1-x}{x^{2}}, x<1, x \\neq 0 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{x^{2}}, x>1\\end{array}\\right.$\r\n\r\nClearly, $f(x)$ is continuous for all $x \\in R$ except at $x=0$\r\n\r\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}\r\n\r\n\\frac{x-2}{x^{3}}, x<1, x \\pm 0 \\\\\r\n\r\n\\frac{2-x}{x^{3}}, x>1\r\n\r\n\\end{array}\\right.$\r\n\r\n$f^{\\prime}(x)>0 \\Rightarrow x<0$ or $1<x<2$\r\n\r\n$f^{\\prime}(x)<0 \\Rightarrow 0<x<1$ or $x>2$\r\n\r\nHence, $f(x)$ is increasing in $(-\\infty, 0) \\cup(1,2)$ and\r\n\r\ndecreasing in (0,1)$\\cup(2, \\infty)$.",
    "image": null
  },
